\title{Staging switches and the default switch set}
\taxon{Design}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\meta{status}{implemented}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\meta{origin}{rlvm-machine/SWITCHES.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} implemented. The default set is the one of the wave-6 integration; \code{eager} and \code{convexpect} were added later on 2026-11-24 and are off.}

\p{\strong{The rule.} Every optimisation of the machine that changes \em{what is computed} sits behind a staging switch: one bit of a per-engine set, tested where the optimisation would act. With no switch the machine is \strong{v0}: the generic tier, the complete procedures, a session of one declaration. A strict engine, which the driver uses for the cold re-check of a rejection, runs with no switch whatever is configured. So there is always a way back, and the differential gates can compare any set with v0 and with the reference engine. One file, \src{rlvm-machine/SWITCHES.md}, is the single list: name, bit, owner, state, and the gate and the measurement that justify the state.}

\p{\strong{Where the stages come from.} The instruction set orders the work as a correctness gate followed by steps, each merged only when its gate is recorded: v0; (1) sessions, gated tabling, shared check facts, expected-type checking; (2) static facts; (3) code blocks for application inference and matching of domains; (4) code blocks for bodies and rules, and case trees; (5) speculative probes; (6) parallel workers. The switches are these steps cut finer. What the measurements did to the plan: the fact records of step 2, the inference blocks of step 3 and the probes of step 5 are built and off, because what is official-exact in them costs more than it saves or gains nothing.}

\p{\strong{How a set is given.} On the launcher \code{\verb~|-X machine.switches=LIST~}. The list is read from left to right starting from the empty set: a name or \code{+name} adds a switch, \code{-name} removes it, \code{none} empties the set, \code{default} adds the default set, \code{all} adds the staging switches of the design except \code{k2}. Without the setting an engine runs the default set. Examples: \code{none} is v0; \code{\verb~|default,-same~}; \code{\verb~|default,eager~}. The module's own tests run with \code{none}, so they do not move when a default changes.}

\p{\strong{The default set}: \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,bodies,pchk,match,joint,code,casetrees,lazycases,directrules,lazyeval,same~}. See [the decision](dec-LZFX) for its gates and numbers.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Switch}\th{What it turns on}\th{State}}
\tr{\td{\code{warm}}\td{a session survives an accepted declaration, see [warm sessions](design-warm-sessions)}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{gated}}\td{evaluation tables a node only if it is shared, a binder body or invariant, or lies deep in an inert nest, see [evaluation and tabling](design-evaluation-and-tabling)}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{bodies}}\td{the body of a beta step and a telescope's domains and results are evaluated without a tabling record}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{expect}, \code{pchk}, \code{match}, \code{joint}}\td{the four [inference shortcuts](design-inference-shortcuts)}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{code}, \code{casetrees}}\td{code blocks for bodies and recursor rules; case trees for the casesOn and matcher shapes, see [code objects and tiers](design-code-objects-and-tiers)}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{lazycases}}\td{the casesOn tree as the unfolding step of lazy delta}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{directrules}}\td{a recursor rule of the plain form is one application of the minor premise}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{lazyeval}, \code{same}}\td{two additions to [conversion](design-conversion)}\td{on}}
\tr{\td{\code{facts}}\td{fact records; the one consumer left is the static answer to "is this a proof"}\td{off: official-exact, costs 1.0 to 2.1 \% of CPU time}}
\tr{\td{\code{probes}}\td{reordered argument comparison under budgets}\td{off: official-exact, costs 1.4 to 3.2 \%}}
\tr{\td{\code{tele}}\td{code blocks for application inference}\td{off: no corpus gains, the Cedar slice loses 2 to 6 \%}}
\tr{\td{\code{k2}}\td{derived rule trees for hot definitions}\td{off, see [the decision](dec-MOCU)}}
\tr{\td{\code{t2}}\td{hot code blocks are offered to the Truffle tier}\td{off, see [the decision](dec-ADIK)}}
\tr{\td{\code{eager}}\td{a declaration that enters [eager reduction](design-eager-reduction) stays on the machine}\td{off; proposed for the default set, see [the decision](dec-X2UF)}}
\tr{\td{\code{convexpect}}\td{expected-type checking of a lambda also when its domain is only convertible with the expected one, as before the typed-binding fix}\td{off: no gain in warm wall clock, see [the decision](dec-0DSI)}}
\tr{\td{\code{levels-complete}}\td{universe levels compared by the complete relation instead of the official algorithm}\td{off by the owner's decision, never a default, see [the decision](dec-P8XP)}}
\tr{\td{\code{m2b}}\td{a direct-mapped memo for applications; one of the experiment bits each package has}\td{off, undecided}}
}

\p{\strong{What is not a switch.} Three things that look like switches are deliberately something else. The rule that declines a declaration entering eager reduction holds in every configuration, v0 included: it is a correctness rule. [The two proof-comparison shortcuts](design-proof-comparison-shortcuts) are behind a system property, so that no switch set, \code{all} included, can turn them on by accident. And the session cap, the tick bound per unit (\code{\verb~|-X machine.unit-ticks~}, default \code{\verb~|2^27~}; it makes the two arena tests on which the official checker does not terminate decline within seconds) and the host depth bound (\code{\verb~|-X machine.hostDepth~}, default 128) are settings with values, each with its own measurement.}

\p{\strong{Changing a default} is a procedure, not an edit. The owner of the switch sends the evidence: difference reports on all 230 arena tests and four corpora at the default cap, and the measurement rows. The integrator re-runs the gate and the mutants on the published tree, asks for open soundness findings, and measures the candidate against the current default in one quiet window, cedar and Mathlib included. The constant, the list and the facade's test change together. The wave-6 integration followed this and still let wrong accepts through, because every gate compared the machine with the reference engine; since then [an adversarial pass with official verdicts](dec-QN1O) comes first.}

\p{\strong{History.} v0 only until 2026-11-24 16:24Z; then \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}; the present set from the wave-6 integration of the same evening. The machine was the default \em{engine} for 54 minutes that evening and is not now, see [that decision](dec-T79V); the default switch set is what runs under \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~}.}

\p{Related: [the gate](design-gate), [the exactness contract](design-exactness-contract). Detail: \src{rlvm-machine/SWITCHES.md}, \src{docs/design/ISA.md} section 11, \src{rlvm-machine/NOTES-INT.md}. Source: [the speed wave](ses-CVTE).}
